    <h1 style="color:#0f1f3d;">Truck Batteries for the Latin American Market</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Latin America follows the BCI standard — Group 31 and 8D sizes to match North American heavy
      trucks — with SAE J537 cold-cranking ratings. A European-heavy fleet means DIN and JIS coverage
      is useful alongside BCI. DINWEYS supplies BCI group sizes on request, with OEM customization.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:20px 0;padding:16px 20px;background:#f4f7fb;border-left:4px solid #f5a623;border-radius:6px;">
      <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.15rem;">Direct Answer</h2>
      <p style="margin:0;">Latin America's heavy-truck fleet is dominated by North American and European makes — Freightliner, Kenworth, International (Navistar), Volvo, Scania, Mercedes-Benz and MAN — so the primary battery group is BCI Group 31 (with legacy 8D and 4D), rated to SAE J537 at &minus;18&deg;C. European OEMs account for roughly three-fifths of commercial-vehicle sales in the region. Climate spans tropical heat (to 40&deg;C in Brazil) and high-altitude Andean cold, and imports clear through country-specific conformity — INMETRO (Brazil), NOM (Mexico), SEC (Chile) — with Santos, Manzanillo and Cartagena as the leading container gateways.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Market Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Industry analysis names Daimler Trucks, Volvo Group, Scania AB, MAN Truck &amp; Bus, Iveco,
      PACCAR and Tata Motors among the leading players in the Latin American truck market
      (<a href="https://www.6wresearch.com/industry-report/latin-america-truck-market-outlook" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6Wresearch</a>).
      Frost &amp; Sullivan data cited by ResearchAndMarkets indicates European OEMs dominate the
      region's commercial-vehicle sales — roughly three-fifths of the total — with Daimler, Traton and
      Volvo as the top three sellers accounting for about two-thirds of market share
      (<a href="https://www.researchandmarkets.com/reports/5695864/latin-american-commercial-vehicle-cv-growth" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ResearchAndMarkets</a>).
      By segment, Isuzu and Hino lead light trucks, Mercedes-Benz Atego, Volkswagen Worker and Hino 500
      appear in medium duty, and Freightliner, Kenworth, Volvo, Scania, International and Mercedes-Benz
      Actros lead heavy duty
      (<a href="https://vecfleet.io/en/blog/popular-cargo-truck-models-latin-america/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VecFleet</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      The precise installed-fleet split by brand is not published in a single official source, so
      treat the mix above as structure rather than exact share. <em>Precise brand share: Not publicly verified.</em>
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Climate &amp; Duty Demands</h2>
    <p>
      Latin America spans several climate zones, and duty demands follow. Northern Brazil and the
      Amazon basin are tropical, with temperatures that can reach 40&deg;C in summer; the central-west
      is tropical savanna with a May&ndash;September dry season and temperatures from 18 to 32&deg;C
      (<a href="https://www.worlddata.info/america/brazil/climate.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WorldData — Brazil climate</a>;
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_of_Brazil" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia — Climate of Brazil</a>).
      The Andes run through Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Bolivia and Chile, where high-altitude and
      high-latitude cold put real cold-cranking demand on batteries — the reason SAE J537 cold-cranking
      ratings tested at &minus;18&deg;C matter.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical consequence for buyers: heat in the tropical north accelerates lead-acid ageing
      and electrolyte water loss, while Andean cold demands dependable cold-cranking current. A
      thick-plate flooded construction covers both, and a sealed AGM option serves fleets that prefer
      maintenance-free operation.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Vehicle Mix &amp; Common Battery Groups</h2>
    <p>
      The group follows the vehicle. North American heavy trucks — Freightliner, Kenworth, International
      — and most regional European trucks use BCI group sizes, principally <strong>Group 31</strong>
      (footprint about 330 &times; 173 &times; 240&nbsp;mm), the modern standard for Class 6&ndash;8 trucks,
      with legacy <strong>8D</strong> and <strong>4D</strong> in some applications
      (<a href="https://altusvolt.com/support/battery-standards/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AltusVolt — battery standards</a>).
      European vehicles may also be specified in DIN/EN codes, and Japanese units in JIS. The groups
      that dominate Latin American fleets:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Battery group</th><th>DINWEYS model</th><th>Capacity / CCA</th><th>Typical vehicle class</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>BCI Group 31</td><td>BCI-31 (on request)</td><td>100 Ah · SAE J537 CCA</td><td>North American Class 6&ndash;8 trucks, buses</td></tr>
        <tr><td>BCI Group 8D / 4D</td><td>on request</td><td>Large legacy sizes</td><td>Heavy trucks, off-highway, marine-adjacent</td></tr>
        <tr><td>JIS N150</td><td>145G51</td><td>135 Ah · 900 A (JIS)</td><td>Japanese trucks (Hino, Isuzu, Fuso)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DIN88 / DIN100</td><td>58827 / 60038</td><td>88 / 100 Ah (EN)</td><td>European trucks (Volvo, Scania, Mercedes, MAN)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="data-note">
      Group-to-vehicle mapping is a third-party fitment reference — a compatible reference, not a
      DINWEYS OEM confirmation. BCI group sizes are supplied to order; confirm dimensions, terminal
      layout and voltage against the specific truck before ordering.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Standards</h2>
    <p>
      Latin America's heavy-truck fleet is dominated by North American and European makes, which use
      BCI (Battery Council International) group sizes — principally Group 31 and 8D — with CCA tested
      to SAE J537 at &minus;18&deg;C. European and Japanese vehicles also appear in some markets, so an
      importer may need DIN or JIS coverage alongside BCI. The three standards are not interchangeable:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Standard</th><th>Issuing body</th><th>CCA test</th><th>Heavy-duty sizes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>BCI</td><td>Battery Council International</td><td>SAE J537 (&minus;18&deg;C)</td><td>Group 31, 8D, 4D</td></tr>
        <tr><td>JIS</td><td>Japanese Industrial Standards</td><td>JIS D5301 (&minus;15&deg;C)</td><td>N150, N200</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DIN/EN</td><td>Deutsches Institut f&uuml;r Normung</td><td>EN 50342-1 (&minus;18&deg;C)</td><td>DIN88, DIN100</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      For a technical comparison, see
      <a href="/compare/jis-vs-din-vs-bci/" style="color:#1e5aa8;">JIS vs DIN vs BCI</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">DINWEYS Relevance</h2>
    <p>
      DINWEYS — manufactured by Chengguang Power Tech Co., Ltd. — carries BCI Group 31 (and legacy 8D/4D
      on request) alongside its core JIS N150/N200 and DIN88/DIN100 lines. BCI sizes are produced to
      order via our BCI production line, so a distributor can specify Group 31 or 8D and get the exact
      size, terminal type and brand their market requires. These are positioned as
      <strong>compatible references</strong> matched by group, dimensions and CCA — not OEM replacements.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Buyer Considerations</h2>
    <ol class="takeaways-list">
      <li><strong>Match the battery to the vehicle and its tray.</strong> BCI group numbers differ from JIS model codes and DIN capacity codes. Confirm group size, dimensions, terminal orientation and voltage against the specific unit.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan for mixed heat and cold.</strong> Tropical heat ages plates faster; Andean high-altitude cold demands dependable cold-cranking. A thick-plate flooded design or sealed AGM covers both ends of the climate range.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear conformity before shipment.</strong> Brazil applies INMETRO certification, Mexico applies NOM electrical-safety standards, and Chile uses SEC approval. Confirm the current scheme and labelling with your importer.</li>
      <li><strong>Specify made-to-order early.</strong> BCI sizes are produced to order, so a drawing-to-sample-to-production cycle applies. Latin American importers consistently value a fast, spec-accurate OEM turnaround with brand and packaging built to order.</li>
    </ol>

