    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What battery standard does Latin America use for trucks?</h3>
      <p>Latin America predominantly follows the BCI standard — Group 31 and 8D sizes — matching North American heavy trucks, with SAE J537 cold-cranking ratings tested at −18°C. European and Japanese vehicles also appear, so DIN and JIS coverage is useful alongside BCI. Match the battery to the vehicle and its tray.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can DINWEYS supply BCI Group 31 and 8D batteries?</h3>
      <p>Yes, made to order via our BCI production line. BCI group sizes carry SAE J537 cold-cranking ratings, and we supply them on request for Latin American and North American fleets, with OEM and private-label customization.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between BCI and JIS sizing?</h3>
      <p>BCI uses group numbers (31, 8D) defined by the Battery Council International for North America and Latin America, with CCA tested to SAE J537 at −18°C. JIS uses model codes (N150, N200) for Asia and Africa, tested to JIS D5301 at −15°C. They are not interchangeable — match the standard to the vehicle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What certification does a battery need to enter Latin American markets?</h3>
      <p>Requirements vary by country. Brazil applies INMETRO certification for regulated products, Mexico applies NOM electrical-safety standards, and Chile uses SEC approval. Confirm the current scheme and labelling with your importer before shipping.</p>
    </div>

